===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|knighten}}, {{m|enm|kniȝten}}, from the noun. Cognate with {{cog|gmh|knehten}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{senseid|en|beknight}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To confer [[knighthood]] upon.
#: {{syn|en|beknight}}
#: {{ux|en|The king '''knighted''' the young [[squire]].}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author=Clayton C. Barbeau|title=Future of the family
|passage=Highborn boys were sent off to another noble household at the age of about seven, to serve strenuously as pages and later as esquires to their lord before they themselves were '''knighted''', looked around for a "lady" and incidentally got married and produced more knightlets, whom they never got to know at all well.}}
# {{lb|en|chess|transitive}} To promote (a [[pawn]]) to a knight.

